# Codex Premium Website Skills Rules

Use these rules when building or reviewing websites, landing pages, portfolios, product UI, frontend components, motion systems, or launch-ready web experiences.

## Operating Standard

- Read the brief before building.
- Infer audience, product category, visual tone, content needs, and interaction depth.
- Make the first viewport visually decisive.
- Avoid generic AI website patterns: centered hero plus three feature cards, vague SaaS copy, decorative blobs, weak buttons, placeholder testimonials, fake metrics, and random gradients.
- Prefer concrete design decisions over vague adjectives.

## Design Direction

- Establish a clear visual direction before implementation.
- Use typography, spacing, color, imagery, material, and layout as a coherent system.
- Use visual assets when the subject benefits from them.
- Keep mobile layouts intentionally composed, not merely stacked.
- Avoid one-note palettes and unsupported decorative effects.

## Frontend Implementation

- Follow the project's existing framework and conventions.
- Prefer project-local dependencies.
- Use semantic structure, accessible controls, responsive constraints, and maintainable components.
- Add stable dimensions for fixed-format UI elements so states do not shift layout.
- Keep text readable and prevent overflow at common viewport sizes.

## Motion

- Use motion to clarify sequence, depth, focus, state, or tactility.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.
- Prefer CSS for simple state transitions, Motion for React state/layout transitions, and GSAP only when timeline or scroll choreography needs it.
- Avoid janky scroll listeners and unnecessary animation on frequent or keyboard-driven actions.

## QA And Handoff

- Inspect rendered output when frontend changes matter.
- Check desktop and mobile viewports.
- Check console/network errors when possible.
- Verify focus states, labels, contrast, reduced motion, and responsive media.
- End with changed files, commands run, checks passed, checks not run, known limitations, and next risks.

## Safety

- Preserve user work.
- Avoid destructive edits unless explicitly requested.
- Create a checkpoint or clear rollback path before risky changes.
- Do not invent proof, metrics, testimonials, logos, or unsupported claims.
